<!--
  The Android 13+ themed-icon layer: the Flow A, in one colour.

  ## Why this is not the foreground with the gradient removed

  It is exactly that, and deliberately so. Same path, same 8-unit stroke, same
  group translate as `ic_launcher_foreground.xml` — so a themed launcher shows
  the same mark in the same place, not a second drawing of it that drifts.
  What changes is only the paint.

  ## Genuinely monochrome

  Android tints this layer with the wallpaper palette, and it does so by
  taking the layer's *alpha* and painting the system's own colour through it.
  A gradient here would not survive that; it would be flattened, and the file
  would be claiming a colour it never gets to keep. So there is one flat
  black, no gradient, and none of the brand hexes appear anywhere below.

  `BrandingResourcesTest` asserts that — no `<gradient>`, no brand colour —
  because "the monochrome icon must genuinely be monochrome" is the kind of
  property that decays the first time somebody copies the foreground over it.

  That the mark survives this at all is a property of the mark: the Flow A is
  one stroked ribbon and carries no colour-dependent meaning, which is why
  BRAND.md ships a `-mono` cut of it.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp" android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108" android:viewportHeight="108"
    android:tint="#FF000000">
    <group android:translateX="21.2" android:translateY="17.8">
        <path
            android:pathData="M 53 56 C 50 41 43 22 32 8 C 22 21 15 36 10 47 C 7.5 52.5 11.5 56.5 15.5 52 C 22 43.5 33 38 45 37.5 C 50 37.3 54 38 57 39"
            android:strokeColor="#FF000000"
            android:strokeWidth="8"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round" />
    </group>
</vector>
